     "text": "{\"count\": 3, \"rationale\": \"The target (an OR) is reachable through any of h3_0, h3_1 or h3_2, so all three must be cut. h3_2 is an OR over h1_0, h1_1 and h2_2. Cutting it without blocking h3_2 itself takes at least 3 blocks. So any solution of size 2 must block h3_2 directly. That leaves 1 block to cut both h3_1 (OR of h2_2 and h1_0) and h3_0 (AND of h1_2 and h1_0). Blocking h1_0 cuts h3_0, but h3_1 stays reachable via h1_2 -> h2_2. Blocking h1_2 cuts h3_0 and h2_2, but h3_1 stays reachable via h1_0. No other single step cuts both, so 2 blocks are impossible. Blocking {h1_0, h1_1, h2_2} works: h3_0 loses h1_0, h3_1 loses both parents, and h3_2 loses all three parents. The minimum is therefore 3.\"}"
